ONE RETAINED EDIT (commit 72c3ba8cf), applied identically to all four record-search loops (found_chicken, found_apple, found_gold, found_silver).

THE MECHANISM WAS READ OFF THE INCREMENT ORDER, not the branch rows. Target's continue block is `addi r25,r25,1` then `addi r27,r27,80` (k first, then the record cursor); ours emitted `addi r26,r26,80` then `addi r24,r24,1` (cursor first). A body-trailing `rec += 0x50` lands at the continue point AFTER the for-increment; only a comma-increment puts it after k++:
-                for (k = 0; k < *(s32*)(world + 0x74); k++) {
+                for (k = 0; k < *(s32*)(world + 0x74); k++, rec += 0x50) {
                     rsub = (s32*)(rec + 4);
                     if (strcmp(lbl_80346F10, (char*)rsub + 0x24) == 0 &&
                         *(s32*)rec == 1 && *rsub == 3) {
                         goto found_chicken;
                     }
-                    rec += 0x50;
                 }
After the edit the aligned view at 0x330 reads `addi r24,r24,1` / `addi r26,r26,80`, matching the target's order exactly at all four loops.

TWO MEASURED NEGATIVES ON THE BRANCH-FUSION AXIS. This is the v1 record's MANDATORY-STEP-1 hypothesis ('make the if-body something other than a bare goto'); both of its named forms were executed and BOTH returned probe.py NEUTRAL-IDENTICAL (object bytes literally unchanged), which is a stronger negative than a regression because the source never reached a codegen decision point.
(a) De Morgan + continue, so the `goto` stops being an if-body and becomes a standalone statement:
